NAME
  /port - Serial port tools: connect, disconnect, list, configure.

STATUS
  stable - covered by termapy's compatibility promise

SYNOPSIS
  /port {name}
  /port -- bare: opens the Port picker in the TUI; elsewhere runs /port.list

DESCRIPTION
  Connected: DEMO @ 115200 8N1
  
  Connect to, disconnect from, list, or configure the serial
  port.  Subcommands cover live signals (DTR/RTS/CTS/DSR/RI/CD),
  USB chip identification (/port.chip.*), and the four mode
  settings baud_rate, byte_size, parity, stop_bits.

AVAILABLE
  TUI: yes   CLI: yes (current)   MCP: yes

SUBCOMMANDS
  /port.baud_rate       stable     Show or set baud rate.
  /port.break           stable     Send a break signal (default 250ms).
  /port.byte_size       stable     Show or set data bits.
  /port.cd              stable     Show Carrier Detect state (read-only).
  /port.chip            stable     Identify USB-serial chip(s) and report USB speed class.
  /port.connect         stable     Connect to the serial port (e.g. /port.connect COM3 9600 N81).
  /port.cts             stable     Show CTS state (read-only).
  /port.disconnect      stable     Disconnect from the serial port.
  /port.dsr             stable     Show DSR state (read-only).
  /port.dtr             stable     Show or set DTR line (hardware only).
  /port.flow_control    stable     Show or set flow control (none/rtscts/xonxoff/manual).
  /port.help            stable     Show /port help.
  /port.info            stable     Print status, params, chip, and live signals for the connected port.
  /port.list            stable     List available serial ports.
  /port.mode            stable     Show or set serial mode (e.g. /port.mode 9600 N81).
  /port.parity          stable     Show or set parity.
  /port.ri              stable     Show Ring Indicator state (read-only).
  /port.rts             stable     Show or set RTS line (hardware only).
  /port.stop_bits       stable     Show or set stop bits.
  /port.usb             stable     Show the whole USB tree, with serial ports marked.
  [166/219] /help port (5.64ms)
demo> /help cfg
NAME
  /cfg - Show or set config values.

STATUS
  stable - covered by termapy's compatibility promise

SYNOPSIS
  /cfg {key {value}}
  /cfg -- bare: opens the Cfg picker in the TUI; elsewhere runs /cfg.list

DESCRIPTION
  Active cfg = demo (2 configs available)
  
  Three modes:
    /cfg                  - TUI: open Cfg picker.  CLI: list configs.
    /cfg baud_rate        - show current value of 'baud_rate'
    /cfg baud_rate 115200 - change it (the TUI asks first)
  
  The value is read as the key's current type (int, float, bool,
  string). Bool accepts:
    on/off/true/false/yes/no/1/0/y/n/t/f
  A key with a fixed set of values (parity, json_style,
  custom_buttons_side, ...) takes one of them in any case.
  A change lasts for this session; to keep it, set it in the
  config editor.
  
  Use /cfg.dump to print every key/value pair as JSON.
  Use /cfg.auto to set values without confirmation (for scripts).

AVAILABLE
  TUI: yes   CLI: yes (current)   MCP: yes

SUBCOMMANDS
  /cfg.auto       stable     Set immediately (no confirmation).
  /cfg.delete     stable     Delete a config and its whole folder (asks first).
  /cfg.dump       stable     Print the config in memory as JSON to the terminal.
  /cfg.edit       stable     Edit the current config file.
  /cfg.explore    stable     Open config directory in file explorer.
  /cfg.file       stable     Print a file from the config folder to the terminal.
  /cfg.help       stable     Show /cfg help.
  /cfg.icon       stable     Create a desktop / menu launcher for the current cfg.
  /cfg.info       stable     Print project summary (tree + config + buttons).
  /cfg.list       stable     List all config files.
  /cfg.load       stable     Load a different config in this session (hot-swap).
  /cfg.loaded     stable     Show what the config load loaded: plugins, symbols, devices.
  /cfg.new        stable     Create a config for a port; /cfg.load <name> switches to it.
  /cfg.rename     stable     Rename a config, folder and file together.
  /cfg.show       stable     Open the current config file in the system viewer.

CONFIGS
