demo> /help sym
NAME
  /sym - Symbol table: name -> address, or address -> name+offset.

STATUS
  preview - works, but may change in any release, outside the compatibility promise

SYNOPSIS
  /sym <addr|name>

DESCRIPTION
  Current symbols = 21 (sym/demo.symbols.json)
  
  Symbols come from your build: /sym.import <file> converts its ELF (names,
  sizes and the C types in its debug info) or its linker map (names and
  sizes) to sym/<cfg>.symbols.json in the config folder (a generated file,
  overwritten on re-import) and loads it.  The sidecar auto-loads whenever
  the config loads -- TUI, CLI and MCP alike.
  
  Address forms:
    0x1000         hex
    1000h          hex (assembly suffix)
    1000           DECIMAL -- bare hex is never guessed
    main           symbol name (exact, case-sensitive)
    main+0x10      symbol plus offset (also main-4)
    tick@adc.c     duplicate static, qualified by file (adc or src/adc.c too)
    name.<bit>     a bit, slice or field (see /help mem); an exact symbol
                   name wins, so count.12 is the symbol count.12
  
  Search forms (/sym.search): the exact name first, else a name
  pattern: plain text is contained (gpio), a wildcard globs (Mon*).
  
  Commands:
    /sym <addr|name>        - name -> address, or address -> name+offset
    /sym.import {file}      - convert an ELF or a linker map and load it
                              (bare: re-import the same file, after a rebuild)
    /sym.load {path}        - reload the sidecar, or load an explicit file
    /sym.unload             - clear the loaded table (file untouched)
    /sym.search <pattern>   - search names
    /sym.info               - file, source, counts by section, range

AVAILABLE
  TUI: yes   CLI: yes (current)   MCP: yes

SUBCOMMANDS
  /sym.import    preview    Convert an ELF or a linker map to sym/<cfg>.symbols.json and load it (bare: re-import the same file).
  /sym.info      preview    Show the loaded symbol table: file, source, counts by section, address range.
  /sym.load      preview    Load a symbol table (default: sym/<cfg>.symbols.json, the file /sym.import writes).
  /sym.search    preview    Search symbols by name: the exact name, else contains (gpio) or a glob (Mon*).
  /sym.unload    preview    Clear the loaded symbol table.
  [173/219] /help sym (6.65ms)
demo> /help mem
NAME
  /mem - Device memory: dump, typed reads, writes and bit ops via /sym names.

STATUS
  preview - works, but may change in any release, outside the compatibility promise

SYNOPSIS
  /mem -- bare: runs /mem.info

DESCRIPTION
  Reads and writes device memory.  Native firmware implements the
  termapy MEM spec (see /help memory):
  
    MEM.R <addr> <len>      -> rows "<ADDR>: <XX XX ...>" then OK
    MEM.W <addr> <hex>      -> OK
    MEM.M <addr> <and> <or> -> the old word, then OK   (atomic masked write)
    MEM.INFO                -> one JSON line then OK
    any failure             -> ERR <reason>
  
  A device with its own peek/poke grammar is described by the profile's
  "memory" block (dialect template).  Targets take every /sym form plus
  bits: gTemp, main+0x10, U1MODE.ON, gFlags.4-6, 0x1000.15.  Bit and
  mask writes use the device's atomic MEM.M when advertised, else a
  read + write-back that can race an ISR (xor always does).
  
  A named target stays inside its symbol: a read's type and a write's
  bytes may not run past its end, and an access: ro symbol (code,
  constants, read-only registers) refuses every write.  A symbol with
  no type reads and writes as the unsigned integer of its size.
  
  Every mutation is audited in the session log (address, word before
  and after, registers touched, origin).  Over MCP they are
  destructive: confirm=true.
  
  Commands:
    /mem.dump <target> {len} {type}  - hexdump; u16/u32 word columns,
                                       i*/f* decimal/float; addr= ascii=
                                       toggle columns (both off = bare)
    /mem.read <target> {type}        - one typed value, field, bit or slice
    /mem.write <target> <value>      - a number at a named symbol or field;
                                       hex bytes at an address or array
    /mem.or|and|xor|clear <target> <mask> - mask ops (clear: word &= ~mask)
    /mem.not <target>                - invert one word
    /mem.str <target> {max}          - NUL-terminated string (default cap 256)
    /mem.info                        - dialect, limits, endian, atomic modify
  
  Bare /mem is /mem.info: it probes the device (MEM.INFO) and shows
  the availability verdict, so it doubles as the check after connect.

AVAILABLE
  TUI: yes   CLI: yes (current)   MCP: yes

SUBCOMMANDS
  /mem.and      preview    AND a mask into one word (atomic via MEM.M when available).
  /mem.clear    preview    Clear mask bits in one word: word &= ~mask (atomic via MEM.M when available).
  /mem.dump     preview    Hexdump or word columns at an address or symbol (default: the symbol's size, else 64).
  /mem.info     preview    Show how memory access is configured: dialect, block limit, address width, endian, atomic modify.
  /mem.not      preview    Invert one word (always read + write-back).
  /mem.or       preview    OR a mask into one word (atomic via MEM.M when available).
  /mem.read     preview    Read one typed value: scalar, char, register field, bit or slice.
  /mem.str      preview    Read a NUL-terminated string at an address or symbol.
  /mem.write    preview    Write a value to a named symbol, register or field, or hex bytes at an address (audited).
  /mem.xor      preview    XOR a mask into one word (always read + write-back).
  [174/219] /help mem (5.69ms)
